@meta
id: 15-05
title: Only part of the file
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-delete
par: 23
minutes: 5
vscode: select from line 14 down, then find-in-selection, then delete each
open: seed.sql
vimrc: set number

@brief
The seed file has two blocks. The staging block at the top deliberately
includes rows for `test_` customers, so QA has something to click on.
The production block, from line 14 down, has picked up the same rows
by copy and paste, and they must not ship.

Delete every `test_` row in the production block. The identical rows in
the staging block stay.

`:g` takes a range in front of it, the same way `:s` did in chapter 14.
With no range it works on the whole file. Give it one and it marks only
the lines inside it.

@keys
| `:14,$g/pattern/d` | from line 14 to the end, delete every line matching `pattern` |
| `:g/pattern/d` | the same with no range: the whole file |

@hints
The pattern alone would delete rows in both blocks. You need to tell
`:g` where to start looking.
--
A range goes before the `g`, exactly where it went before the `s` in
chapter 14. `14,$` is "line 14 to the end".
--
`:14,$g/test_/d` and Enter. Four rows go from production; the four in
staging are untouched.

@solution
    :14,$g/test_/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes, no counting, no selecting.

Everything that worked as a range in chapter 14 works here: two line
numbers, `.` for the current line, `$` for the last, `%` for the whole
file, and a pattern to find the boundary rather than a number. So
`:/^-- production/,$g/test_/d` would do the same job without you needing
to know that the block starts on line 14. Use whichever you can say
faster.

The default range for `:g` is `%`, the whole file. That is the opposite
of `:s`, whose default is the current line, and it is the single most
common surprise for people who have just learned substitute. `:s/a/b/`
touches one line. `:g/a/d` touches every line in the file. Keep the
asymmetry in mind and it will not bite you.

@start seed.sql
-- staging
INSERT INTO customer (id, email, tier) VALUES ('cust_10', 'ana@example.com', 'basic');
INSERT INTO customer (id, email, tier) VALUES ('cust_11', 'ben@example.com', 'premium');
INSERT INTO customer (id, email, tier) VALUES ('test_01', 'qa-one@globex.test', 'basic');
INSERT INTO customer (id, email, tier) VALUES ('test_02', 'qa-two@globex.test', 'premium');
INSERT INTO "order" (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_4001', 'cust_10', 'PENDING', 4999);
INSERT INTO "order" (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_4002', 'cust_11', 'SHIPPED', 12000);
INSERT INTO "order" (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_4003', 'test_01', 'PENDING', 100);
INSERT INTO "order" (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_4004', 'test_02', 'CANCELLED', 100);
INSERT INTO payment (id, order_id, amount) VALUES ('pay_9001', 'ord_4001', 4999);
INSERT INTO payment (id, order_id, amount) VALUES ('pay_9002', 'ord_4002', 12000);

-- production
